### Does the issue also appear in Chromium / Google Chrome?

No

### Expected Behavior

`deprecate.removeFunction(fn, name)` is an internal helper that wraps a function slated for removal so that calling it emits a deprecation warning exactly once. Like its siblings `deprecate.renameFunction` and `deprecate.moveAPI`, it is typed to preserve the wrapped function's signature (`<T extends Function>(fn: T, removedName: string): T`). Callers should therefore get back the deprecated function's return value when invoking the wrapper.

### Actual Behavior

The wrapper calls `fn.apply(this, arguments)` but does not `return` the result, so the wrapper always returns `undefined` regardless of what the deprecated function returned. This diverges from `renameFunction` and `moveAPI`, which do `return fn.apply(...)`, and silently violates the wrapper's type signature.

Relevant code — [`lib/common/deprecate.ts` lines 50–61](https://github.com/electron/electron/blob/main/lib/common/deprecate.ts#L50-L61):

```ts
export function removeFunction<T extends Function>(fn: T, removedName: string): T {
  if (!fn) {
    throw new Error(`'${removedName} function' is invalid or does not exist.`);
  }

  // wrap the deprecated function to warn user
  const warn = warnOnce(`${fn.name} function`);
  return function (this: any) {
    warn();
    fn.apply(this, arguments);   // <-- no `return`
  } as unknown as typeof fn;
}
```

Minimal reproduction of the divergence between the two wrappers:

```js
const deprecate = require('electron/main').deprecate ??
  require('./lib/common/deprecate');

function oldFn (a, b) { return a + b; }

const removed = deprecate.removeFunction(oldFn, 'oldFn');
const renamed = deprecate.renameFunction(oldFn, 'newFn');

console.log(removed(2, 3));  // undefined  (should be 5)
console.log(renamed(2, 3));  // 5
```

